{"record": "end", "run_id": "20261116T081941Z_dev-02_flat_dbc7", "reason": "answered", "detail": "", "answer": {"answer": "No. The two analyses use different likelihood-ratio thresholds. They also profile over different nuisance parameters.\n\n- **dSph gamma-ray limits:** The limit is one-sided. It is set where 2[ln L_max − ln L(⟨σv⟩_95)] = 2.71, i.e. a TS decrease of 2.71 or a log-likelihood drop of 1.355. The nuisance parameter profiled over is the J-factor of each dwarf. It enters as J = s·J0 with a Gaussian penalty on log10 s, whose width is the quoted uncertainty σ_J in log10 J. The profile runs over log10 s in [−3, 3].\n\n- **AMS-02 antiproton limits:** The limit is the upper endpoint of the ΔQ ≤ 3.84 region, where Q = −2 ln L and ΔQ is measured relative to its minimum over non-negative ⟨σv⟩. This is the nominal two-sided 95% threshold for one parameter of interest. The paper says it differs from the usual one-sided 2.71 prescription. The nuisance parameter profiled over is the diffusive-halo half-height L. It carries a Gaussian penalty in log10 L, centred on 4.96 kpc with σ = 0.197, and is profiled over 1 ≤ L/kpc ≤ 12.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "4.2"}, {"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "5"}]}, "turns": 3, "seconds": 9.287, "tool_calls": 2, "tool_result_chars": 294172, "input_tokens": 8, "output_tokens": 666, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 129197, "cache_read_input_tokens": 67415, "parser_versions": ["4"], "response_models": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "ended_at": "2026-11-16T08:19:50+00:00"}
